 { A pawn down in a position with play on both flanks is usually a matter of concern. How can Black change this scenario? } 1... { -KEY- } a5 $1 { Preparing ...Rb3 and ...a5-a4-a3 in order to swap pawns and reduce the battle to one single flank. Due to his passively placed king, White can't prevent this plan. } 2. g4 a4 3. Kg3 Rb3 4. h4 h6 5. h5 a3 $1 6. bxa3 Rxa3 7. Rd6+ Kf7 8. Kf4 { We saw a similar ending in Example 156 (Volkov-Rublevsky) in Chapter Three, where Black successfully applied a rear attack with his rook. In this game Gurevich will show an alternative defensive plan. } Rb3 9. Ke4 Rb4+ 10. Ke5 Rb5+ 11. Rd5 Rb7 $1 { A typical method. Black keeps the the vital seventh rank controlled by his rook, so that the king can stay on f7. Only when White's king moves away from his fellow pawns will the rook switch angle. } 12. f4 Re7+ 13. Kf5 Ra7 14. Rb5 ( { Here or later, the advance } 14. g5 { can be met by } hxg5 15. fxg5 g6 $1 16. hxg6 Kg7 { just like later on in the game. } ) 
Re7 15. Rc5 Ra7 16. Rd5 Rb7 17. Ke4 Ke6 18. Re5+ Kf7 19. g5 { There is nothing better. } Rb4+ 20. Kf5 hxg5 21. fxg5 g6+ $1 { A nice defensive trick, one that is familiar to us from Example 164 (Erenburg-Ju). } 22. hxg6+ Kg7 23. Re7+ Kg8 24. g7 Rb6 25. Ra7 Rc6 26. Kg4 Rb6 27. Kh5 Rc6 28. g6 Rc7 $1 29. Rxc7 { Stalemate. } 
