 ( { c) Interestingly, going for pawn exchanges on the opposite flank with } 1... b4 $6 { is less advisable here, since Black would lose his target on c3 and let White's rook activate itself after } 2. cxb4 Rxb4 3. Ra3 { In such a rook ending with a four vs. three pawns on one flank, the current black kingside structure is far from the ideal f7-g6-h5 chain that we saw inExample 458 (Skripchenko-Vasilevich), among others. } ) 
2. fxg6 ( { Or } 2. Kf3 h5 $1 { insisting on the idea; e.g. } 3. Kg3 hxg4 4. hxg4 gxf5 5. gxf5 Re4 6. Rd5 b4 { and Black should make a draw - Sutovsky. } ) 
fxg6 3. Kd2 b4 $1 { Now that White's pawn structure has deteriorated, the moment is right for a battle on one single flank. } 4. cxb4 Rxb4 5. Rd6 h5 $1 { In the same spirit as before, reducing the number of pawns on the board. } 6. Rxg6 hxg4 7. hxg4 ( { Obviously, } 7. Rxg4 Rxg4 8. hxg4 Ke6 { is nothing. } ) 
Re4 $1 8. Rg5 Ke6 
