 ( { In the previous example Black had no choice but to accept the exchange of pawns proposed by the opponent. Here, in contrast, he rejects it - an unusual decision but essential on this occasion.White is about to win a pawn - Black can't cover the b-pawn without losing track of the f3-pawn. It would seem natural to play } 1. b6 hxg4 { in the first place, reducing the total number of pawns on the board. However, Berg notices that the h-pawn will play a crucial role in his future counterplay. } ) 
 { -KEY- } Ra8 $1 { Active defence. } 2. Rxb4 ( 2. gxh5 Kh6 { would let Black attack the h3-pawn very soon. } ) 
 ( 2. Rd2 Rf8 { isn't productive for White either, when ...Rf4 turns up as an additional idea for Black. } ) 
Rxa2+ 3. Kxf3 h4 $1 { The key move of this endgame, which secures a future target on h3. } ( { In contrast, accepting the pawn exchange with } 3... hxg4 $6 { would make Black's task more difficult after } 4. hxg4 { , followed by a king's transfer to the opposite flank - Furhoff. } ) 
4. Ke4 Rh2 5. Kf5 Rxh3 6. Kxg5 { A pawn exchange was effected after all - but rather in Black's favour, thanks to the counterplay provided by the passed h-pawn. } Rh1 7. Rb7+ Kf8 8. Kg6 Re1 $1 { Berg intends to swap the h-pawn for White's b-pawn in order to save himself in a rook and pawn vs. rook ending. } ( { Inferior was } 8... h3 $2 9. g5 h2 10. Rb8 Ke7 11. Rh8 $1 Kd6 12. Rh5 Kc5 13. Kh6 Kb4 14. g6 { etc. } ) 
9. Rf7+ ( { a) After } 11. Rh7 Re6 12. Kg5 Rb6 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 11. g5 Re7 $1 12. Rb4 Re6 $1 13. Kf5 Re3 14. Rxh4 Rxb3 15. Kg6 Kg8 { , Black achieves his goal. } ) 
Kg8 10. Rf3 Rg1 $1 11. Kg5 ( { On } 11. g5 { , Black had } Rg3 $1 { prepared. } ) 
Rh1 $1 { With the king temporarily out of danger, the moment is right to bet on the passed pawn. } 12. Kh5 ( { Or } 12. b4 h3 13. b5 h2 14. Rh3 Rb1 15. Rxh2 Rxb5 ) 
h3 13. Kh4 h2 14. Kh3 Rg1 $1 15. Kxh2 Rxg4 16. Kh3 ( { After } 16. Rg3 Rxg3 17. Kxg3 Kf7 ( { and } 17. Kh3 Kg7 { with remote opposition is also possible. } ) 
18. Kf3 Ke7 19. Ke3 Kd7 20. Kd3 Kc7 21. Kc3 Kb7 $1 { Black draws - Furhoff. } ) 
Rg7 { Interestingly, both kings are now cut off. } 17. Rf4 Rb7 $1 { Anticipating a pawn ending, Berg provokes b3-b4, which will leave the related key squares at a greater distance from White's king. } 18. b4 Rf7 $1 { We are familiar with such bridges from Chapter Three. } 19. Rc4 ( { Or } 19. Kg4 Rxf4 20. Kxf4 Kf8 $1 { with a draw, now that the b-pawn has left b3. } ) 
